# SAFe 6 Evaluation Checklist (Tuckman Model)

This evaluation checklist aligns **SAFe 6 events and practices** with the **Tuckman Model Stages of Team Development** (*Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning/Transforming*). Use this matrix as a Scrum Master / Agile Coach to diagnose team maturity, facilitate SAFe cadences effectively, and guide your team toward high performance.

---

##  Team Evaluation Matrix

| Tuckman Stage | SAFe 6 Event / Focus Area | Evaluation Checklist & Assessment Questions | Coaching Focus & Actionable Steps |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1. Forming**<br>*(Orientation & Dependency)* | **PI Planning** | - [ ] Are team members hesitant or looking to the SM/RTE for every decision?<br>- [ ] Are PI Objectives vague or overly reliant on external guidance?<br>- [ ] Is capacity planning overly conservative or purely guess-driven? | • Clarify SAFe roles, ART vision, and team boundaries.<br>• Provide structured templates for PI Objectives and risk boards.<br>• Protect the team from initial external pressure. |
| | **Iteration Planning** | - [ ] Does the team struggle to define a clear Iteration Goal?<br>- [ ] Are user stories accepted without asking clarifying questions?<br>- [ ] Are story point estimates inconsistent across team members? | • Teach SAFe normalized story point estimation.<br>• Guide the team on breaking down features into small, testable user stories.<br>• Facilitate discussions to ensure equal participation. |
| | **Daily Sync (Standup)** | - [ ] Are updates reported directly to the SM/PO instead of peers?<br>- [ ] Is there silence when asking if anyone needs help?<br>- [ ] Do team members focus strictly on individual tasks rather than team goals? | • Redirect status updates back to the whole team.<br>• Ask: *"How does today's work move us closer to our Iteration Goal?"*<br>• Keep the event structured and under 15 minutes. |
| **2. Storming**<br>*(Conflict & Friction)* | **Backlog Refinement** | - [ ] Are there frequent arguments over technical approach or estimates?<br>- [ ] Is the Product Owner dominating requirements without technical input?<br>- [ ] Is technical debt or data architecture causing frustration? | • Enforce a strict **Definition of Ready (DoR)**.<br>• Use silent voting or Planning Poker to surface hidden disagreements.<br>• Coach the PO to share the business *why* and listen to technical *how*. |
| | **System Demo** | - [ ] Is there anxiety or resistance to showing unpolished work to stakeholders?<br>- [ ] Does negative feedback cause defensiveness or finger-pointing?<br>- [ ] Are teams blaming external dependencies for incomplete features? | • Reframe the System Demo as a feedback mechanism, not a test.<br>• Mediate conflict constructively during post-demo discussions.<br>• Celebrate partial progress to build psychological safety. |
| | **Iteration Retrospective** | - [ ] Is the meeting silent, overly defensive, or full of personal complaints?<br>- [ ] Are the same problems brought up sprint after sprint without solutions?<br>- [ ] Do retro action items lack assigned owners or clear completion dates? | • Use structured retro formats (e.g., *Sailboat*, *4 Ls*) to focus on systemic issues.<br>• Limit retro improvement items to **1–2 high-impact actions** per iteration.<br>• Follow up publicly on previous action items at the start of every retro. |
| **3. Norming**<br>*(Cohesion & Alignment)* | **PI Planning & ART Sync** | - [ ] Does the team proactively negotiate dependencies on the ART Planning Board?<br>- [ ] Are team PI Objectives realistic, measurable, and tied to business value?<br>- [ ] Does the team collaborate smoothly with System Architecture and Product Management? | • Step back and allow the team to drive cross-team dependency discussions.<br>• Focus on refining Business Value scoring with Business Owners.<br>• Support peer coaching and cross-functional skill sharing. |
| | **Iteration Planning** | - [ ] Does the team consistently commit to realistic capacity based on historical velocity?<br>- [ ] Is work balanced across development, testing, and documentation?<br>- [ ] Is technical debt deliberately built into every iteration backlog? | • Encourage team-led story ownership rather than SM assignment.<br>• Introduce automation and Built-in Quality practices into acceptance criteria.<br>• Maintain a consistent delivery cadence. |
| | **Daily Sync (Standup)** | - [ ] Does the team self-organize around blockers without SM intervention?<br>- [ ] Is the conversation focused on flow, WIP limits, and the Iteration Goal?<br>- [ ] Do team members actively jump in to help finish stuck stories? | • Shift to a "walk the board" approach (focusing on work items from right to left).<br>• Monitor Work in Process (WIP) limits together.<br>• Step out of the active facilitation role and let the team lead. |
| **4. Performing**<br>*(Autonomy & High Flow)* | **Inspect & Adapt (I&A)** | - [ ] Does the team actively participate in the Problem-Solving Workshop using root-cause analysis?<br>- [ ] Are metrics (e.g., Flow Velocity, Flow Predictability) analyzed objectively?<br>- [ ] Does the team propose systemic improvements for the entire Agile Release Train (ART)? | • Empower the team to lead ART-level process improvements.<br>• Focus coaching on lean metrics, value stream optimization, and flow engineering.<br>• Support team autonomy and experiment-driven innovation. |
| | **System Demo & Release** | - [ ] Is integrated working software/product demonstrated smoothly every iteration?<br>- [ ] Is the team deploying frequently using continuous integration and delivery (CI/CD)?<br>- [ ] Does the team collect real-time customer/data feedback post-release? | • Help the team optimize the Delivery Pipeline (DevOps / MLOps).<br>• Connect team outcomes directly to customer metrics and OKRs.<br>• Showcase team success across the organization. |
| **5. Adjourning / Transforming**<br>*(Transition & Evolution)* | **ART Re-mapping / Team Re-organization** | - [ ] Is the team preparing for a SAFe value stream re-mapping or structural shift?<br>- [ ] Are knowledge transfer plans and documentation up to date?<br>- [ ] Is there anxiety or sadness regarding team changes or project completion? | • Facilitate closure sessions to celebrate team achievements and learnings.<br>• Ensure continuous documentation and artifact handoff.<br>• Guide team members into their new SAFe teams or value streams with confidence. |

---

##  Quick Tips for Scrum Masters

1. **Match your coaching style to the stage:**
   - **Forming:** Directing & Teaching
   - **Storming:** Coaching & Mediating
   - **Norming:** Facilitating & Supporting
   - **Performing:** Delegating & Empowering
2. **Focus on SAFe Core Values:** Align team behavior with *Alignment, Transparency, Respect for People, and Relentless Improvement*.
3. **Track Flow Metrics:** Use the SAFe 6 Flow Framework (*Flow Velocity, Flow Time, Flow Predictability, Flow Efficiency*) to measure real operational progress rather than feelings.
